\subsection{Per-Task Analysis}
\label{sec:per_task}

Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla, leaving minimal room for improvement; measured effects concentrate on three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6) where Vanilla CAR falls below 0.58. The overall CAR of 0.780 is dominated by these three hard tasks, which average 0.342 compared to 0.968 for the easy tasks (full breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}).

\textbf{Detach is systematically worst on hard tasks.} Detach achieves the lowest CAR on T3 (0.11 vs.\ 0.31--0.38 for other conditions) and T5 (0.05 vs.\ 0.07--0.23), consistent with gradient detachment exacerbating degradation where obstacle proximity is tight. Among gradient-flow conditions, no consistent ordering emerges: on T3, Vanilla (0.38) matches Full OCC (0.37); on T6, Full OCC (0.61) leads while Random OCC (0.55) trails. This reinforces the damage-prevention interpretation---gradient flow prevents Detach-specific degradation but does not systematically improve clearance.

\subsection{Temporal Dynamics and Reference Frame}
\label{sec:temporal}

\textbf{Implementation detail and temporal observations.} In cutoff conditions, setting the auxiliary loss weight to zero does \emph{not} disable the auxiliary head's forward pass; its BatchNorm2d layers continue updating running statistics, creating Detach-mode interference post-cutoff. Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports five cutoff variants spanning 15K--45K gradient flow steps. Mid-training transitions produce outcomes that are lower on average and more seed-dependent than consistent regimes ($\sigma_\text{CAR}$: Full OCC 0.032, Detach 0.002 vs.\ elevated cutoff variability). The key observation is that gradient flow \emph{continuity}---whether consistently present or absent---yields more predictable outcomes than mid-training transitions. Per-task data reveal task-specific instability: on T7 and T9 (stable at $\geq$0.94 under all consistent regimes), cutoff conditions exhibit per-seed swings of 60--70\,pp (e.g., Reverse Cutoff T7: 0.30 vs.\ 1.00).

\textbf{Representation-fragile tasks (preliminary).} Per-task analysis reveals that withdrawal selectively destabilizes \emph{representation-fragile} tasks (T7, T9)---reliably solved under stable training (CAR $\geq$0.94 across all Vanilla and Detach seeds) but showing extreme per-seed divergence under mid-training transitions. Tasks solvable by generic motor primitives (T0--T2, T4, T8; CAR generally $\geq$0.88 across gradient-flow conditions) or beyond model capacity (T5) show stable CAR regardless of training regime. Cutoff@35K (3 seeds) achieves CAR 0.779---statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla ($\Delta = -$0.1\,pp), confirming that 35K steps of gradient flow fully protects collision avoidance despite 15K steps of post-cutoff BN interference.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla despite richer spatial content.} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR of 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039---identical to Vanilla (0.780 $\pm$ 0.012)---despite providing per-pixel geometric information directly aligned with collision avoidance. Three distinct label types (structured BEV occupancy, random binary maps, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained, consistent with gradient flow presence being the primary factor within the variance of our experiments. Depth exhibits the highest cross-seed CAR variance among gradient-flow conditions ($\sigma = 0.039$), with hard-task ranges of 0.20--0.78 (T3) and 0.16--0.84 (T6), consistent with auxiliary supervision increasing seed sensitivity.

