\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_gradient}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Gradient norms, CKA, normalization ablation, and behavioral metrics converge on a dual-mechanism account. The Detach+LN ablation decomposes the 3.4\,pp Detach deficit into BatchNorm interference (1.9\,pp) and gradient flow absence (1.5\,pp). These are mechanistically distinct: Detach+LN fully recovers pretrained representations (CKA $\approx$ Vanilla) despite \texttt{stop\_gradient}, conclusively identifying BatchNorm as the sole cause of representational drift. The residual 1.5\,pp gap---a representation-behavior dissociation---indicates that gradient flow provides modest but consistent behavioral regularization beyond representation preservation, what we term \emph{existence regularization}: gradient presence alone, regardless of direction or label content, prevents collapse.

We observe that under Detach, SigLIP drifts more than DINOv2 (CKA 0.312 vs.\ 0.539). SigLIP's cooperative gradient alignment in late layers (19--25\% conflict) creates vulnerability when auxiliary flow is removed; DINOv2's orthogonal structure ($\sim$50\% conflict) confers resilience. LayerNorm fully resolves this asymmetry (Detach+LN: both encoders CKA $\geq$ 0.96).

\textbf{Practical guideline.} When attaching an auxiliary head during VLA fine-tuning: (1)~maintain gradient flow through the encoder, and (2)~use LayerNorm rather than BatchNorm in the auxiliary head. No ground-truth spatial labels are required; the head is discarded at zero inference cost. This training-time intervention complements runtime safety methods~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}.

\textbf{Limitations.} Single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT, LoRA rank 32), single benchmark (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks, static obstacles). The effect size among gradient-flow conditions is modest (within cross-seed variance); the robust finding is the Detach degradation ($-$3.4\,pp). Gradient and CKA analyses use cross-seed checkpoints; same-seed replication is in progress. Cutoff conditions have 1--3 seeds, limiting temporal conclusions to preliminary observations.

